# LLM-Reviewer Project Rules

## Project Context
Research prototype for LLM-based peer review simulation using the ICLR Gen-Review SQLite database.
Focus: 2023 papers with binary Accept/Reject decisions — comparing LLM-generated review scores
against human reviewer scores and GPT-4o (GENAI_REVIEW) baselines.

## Architecture
- `src/reviewer_sim/` — Main package (PYTHONPATH=src)
  - `ingest/` — Data loading from SQLite → JSONL
  - `generate/` — LLM review providers (mock, llamacpp, together)
  - `evaluate/` — Comparison metrics (text-based, score-based, decision-based)
  - `utils/` — Config, helpers
- `tests/` — Pytest test suite (mirrors src structure)
- `data/gen_review.db` — Source SQLite (~1.5GB, gitignored)
- `data/processed/` — Exported JSONL slices
- `notebooks/` — EDA and analysis

## Database Schema (Key Tables)
- SUBMISSION: id, paper_number, title, abstract, tldr, primary_area, decision, when_submitted
- REVIEW: paper_id, reviewer_id, summary, rating, confidence, correctness,
  technical_novelty_and_significance, empirical_novelty_and_significance,
  main_review, summary_of_the_review, binocular_score
  NOTE: For 2023 papers, main_review is EMPTY. Use summary_of_the_review for review text.
  NOTE: For 2023, soundness/presentation/contribution/strength/weaknesses/questions are EMPTY.
  Available numeric fields for 2023: rating, confidence, correctness,
  technical_novelty_and_significance, empirical_novelty_and_significance
- GENAI_REVIEW: paper_id, type (neutral/positive/negative), generated, rating, binocular_score
  These are GPT-4o (ChatPDF) reviews — use type='neutral' as the primary baseline.

## JSONL Schema Convention (2023 slice)
All exports for the 2023 slice MUST use this schema so that evaluate() works:
  {
    "paper_id": "...",
    "title": "...",
    "abstract": "...",
    "decision_binary": "Accept" | "Reject",
    "human_review": {
      "text": "<from summary_of_the_review>",
      "rating": <int 1-10>,
      "confidence": <int 1-5>,
      "correctness": <int 1-4>,
      "technical_novelty": <int 1-4>,
      "empirical_novelty": <int 1-4>
    },
    "gpt4o_baseline": {
      "text": "<from GENAI_REVIEW.generated where type='neutral'>",
      "rating": <int, parsed from GENAI_REVIEW.rating>
    },
    ...meta...
  }
This ensures backward compatibility: evaluate() reads human_review.text for TF-IDF,
and new score-based metrics read from human_review.rating etc.

## Provider Pattern
Generators implement: def generate(self, example: Dict) -> Dict
- MockGenerator: deterministic, for testing
- LlamaCppGenerator: local GGUF models
- TogetherGenerator: Together AI API (async, uses /v1/chat/completions)
All return {"text": "...", "score": <int>, ...optional score fields...}

## Development Practices
- Test-driven: write tests BEFORE implementation. Tests go in tests/ mirroring src structure.
- All exports must be deterministic (seed-based sampling).
- Use PYTHONPATH=src for all commands.
- Parse ratings with regex: strings like "8: Top 50%..." → extract leading int.
- Handle missing data gracefully (per-example error handling, pipeline continues on failures).
- Config via environment variables for Docker/CI compatibility.
- Never modify the existing enrich_primary_area.py workflow.

## Rating Scales (ICLR 2023)
- rating: 1-10 (overall recommendation)
- confidence: 1-5
- correctness: 1-4
- technical_novelty_and_significance: 1-4
- empirical_novelty_and_significance: 1-4

## Key Environment Variables
- TOGETHER_API_KEY: Required for TogetherGenerator and enrich_primary_area
- MODEL_PROVIDER: mock | llamacpp | together
- MODEL_PATH: Required for llamacpp
- TOGETHER_MODEL: Model ID for Together AI (default: meta-llama/Llama-3-8b-chat-hf)